Data engineering with MySQL: From fundamentals to real-world patterns
Foundations: How relational databases fit into data engineering
Database as a system of record: In applications (e.g., ecommerce, HR, logistics), MySQL stores authoritative, transactional data—customers, orders, employees. This “OLTP” layer prioritizes correctness and concurrency.

Tables and schemas as contracts: Each table’s columns, types, and keys define the shape of data. In data engineering, these are the upstream contracts your pipelines depend on.

Normalization vs. analytics needs: OLTP schemas are normalized to reduce duplication; analytics needs often favor denormalized or dimensional schemas (star/snowflake) to speed up queries.

Real-world scenario
Company: Online marketplace in Hyderabad.

OLTP (MySQL): customers, orders, order_items tables power the website.

Data engineering pipeline: Nightly jobs (or CDC streams) extract from MySQL, stage/clean, then load to a warehouse (e.g., BigQuery/Snowflake) for dashboards and ML features.

Transactional schema design and constraints
Primary keys ensure uniqueness of entities (e.g., customer_id, order_id).

Foreign keys enforce relationships (order belongs to a customer), preventing orphan records.

Indexes speed up reads; choose them carefully to balance write performance.

Data types matter (DECIMAL for currency, DATE/TIMESTAMP for time series).

sql
-- Core OLTP tables
CREATE TABLE customers (
  customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
  name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
  city VARCHAR(50),
  email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE
);

CREATE TABLE orders (
  order_id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
  customer_id INT NOT NULL,
  amount DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
  order_date DATETIME NOT NULL,
  status ENUM('PENDING','PAID','CANCELLED') NOT NULL,
  FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(customer_id)
);

CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_date ON orders(customer_id, order_date);
Data engineering correlation
Quality and lineage: Constraints reduce bad data upstream. Fewer fixes downstream.

Index choices: Pipelines that filter by order_date or customer_id will be faster with compound indexes.

Schema evolution: Adding columns (e.g., status) requires backward-compatible pipeline updates and versioned schemas.

Joins in real life: Building business views from normalized data
Normalization → multiple tables: You’ll join to answer business questions.

Join types support different use cases:

INNER JOIN: Only matched entities (e.g., customers who placed orders).

LEFT JOIN: Preserve a master list (e.g., marketing wants all customers, even inactive).

RIGHT JOIN: Preserve the secondary list (less common in OLTP design).

FULL OUTER JOIN: Simulated in MySQL via UNION (useful in reconciliation).

sql
-- Customers with their orders (matched only)
SELECT c.customer_id, c.name, o.order_id, o.amount, o.order_date
FROM customers c
INNER JOIN orders o
  ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.status = 'PAID'
ORDER BY o.order_date DESC;

-- All customers, whether they have orders or not (retention analysis)
SELECT c.customer_id, c.name, o.order_id, o.amount
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o
  ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
ORDER BY c.customer_id;
Real-world scenario
Activation funnel: Product analytics needs “customers without orders” (LEFT JOIN) to compute first-purchase conversion.

Revenue reporting: Finance extracts “paid orders” (INNER JOIN) to reconcile monthly revenue with payment gateway logs.

Data reconciliation: To detect orphan orders (data issue), use RIGHT JOIN to find orders missing customers and investigate upstream integrity.

Aggregations and time windows: Turning rows into insights
Aggregations answer business questions: totals, averages, distributions.

GROUP BY segments data: by customer, city, product, week.

Windowing supports trends: daily/weekly/monthly rollups.

sql
-- Total revenue and paid orders per day (for daily dashboard)
SELECT DATE(order_date) AS order_day,
       COUNT(*) AS orders_count,
       SUM(amount) AS revenue
FROM orders
WHERE status = 'PAID'
GROUP BY DATE(order_date)
ORDER BY order_day DESC;

-- Customer lifetime value (LTV) and number of purchases
SELECT c.customer_id,
       c.name,
       COUNT(o.order_id) AS purchase_count,
       COALESCE(SUM(o.amount), 0) AS total_spent
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o
  ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
  AND o.status = 'PAID'
GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.name
ORDER BY total_spent DESC;
Data engineering correlation
Daily batch jobs: A pipeline materializes these aggregates into summary tables for BI tools (e.g., daily_revenue, customer_ltv).

Slowly changing dimensions: Customer attributes (city, segment) may change; pipelines capture changes to keep aggregates historically accurate.

Feature stores: Aggregations (e.g., purchase_count_last_30_days) feed recommendation models.

Pipelines, staging, and data quality: Making OLTP analytics-ready
Staging layer: Raw extracts land in a “staging” schema; minimal transformations applied first (type casts, dedup).

Clean layer: Business rules applied (filter cancelled, map statuses, standardize dates).

Dimensional layer: Build star schemas for analytics performance (facts + dimensions).

sql
-- Example: Staging to clean revenue fact
-- staging.orders_raw -> clean.fct_orders
CREATE TABLE clean.fct_orders AS
SELECT
  order_id,
  customer_id,
  CAST(amount AS DECIMAL(10,2)) AS amount,
  CAST(order_date AS DATETIME) AS order_date,
  status
FROM staging.orders_raw
WHERE status IN ('PAID','PENDING');  -- filter corrupt statuses

-- Dimensional modeling (simplified)
CREATE TABLE dim_customers AS
SELECT customer_id, name, city
FROM customers;

CREATE TABLE fct_orders_daily AS
SELECT DATE(order_date) AS order_day,
       customer_id,
       SUM(amount) AS revenue,
       COUNT(*) AS orders_count
FROM clean.fct_orders
WHERE status = 'PAID'
GROUP BY DATE(order_date), customer_id;
Real-world scenario
Marketing dashboards: Use fct_orders_daily joined with dim_customers to analyze city-wise revenue trends.

Data quality checks: Row counts, null checks, referential integrity tests run after each load. If orders increases but revenue drops unexpectedly, pipelines alert engineers.

Schema drift handling: If the app adds discount_amount, pipelines evolve: stage the new column, update transformations, adjust aggregates.

Performance, scale, and reliability: Practical choices in MySQL
Indexes: Essential for query speed; use selective columns, composite indexes for common predicates (customer_id + order_date).

Partitions: For large tables, partition by date to speed up scans and simplify retention.

Upserts (MERGE-like): Use INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE to maintain aggregates or dimension keys.

Incremental loads: Use order_date or an updated_at column for CDC and delta processing.

Transactions: Wrap multi-step writes to maintain consistency during ETL.

Backups and replication: Ensure read replicas for reporting and point-in-time recovery for safety.

sql
-- Upsert daily revenue snapshot (idempotent load)
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS daily_revenue (
  revenue_day DATE PRIMARY KEY,
  orders_count INT NOT NULL,
  revenue DECIMAL(12,2) NOT NULL
);

INSERT INTO daily_revenue (revenue_day, orders_count, revenue)
SELECT DATE(order_date) AS revenue_day,
       COUNT(*) AS orders_count,
       SUM(amount) AS revenue
FROM orders
WHERE status = 'PAID'
GROUP BY DATE(order_date)
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE
  orders_count = VALUES(orders_count),
  revenue = VALUES(revenue);

-- Partition orders by month (example)
ALTER TABLE orders
PARTITION BY RANGE (YEAR(order_date)*100 + MONTH(order_date)) (
  PARTITION p2023_01 VALUES LESS THAN (202301),
  PARTITION p2023_02 VALUES LESS THAN (202302),
  PARTITION pMAX     VALUES LESS THAN MAXVALUE
);
Data engineering correlation
Idempotency: Upserts ensure reruns don’t double-count.

Cost/performance: Partitions + indexes limit scanned data for daily jobs.

Operational separation: Read replicas run heavy analytics queries without impacting the OLTP app.

Putting it all together: An end-to-end mini project
Business goal
Build a daily revenue and customer LTV dashboard for an ecommerce app, powered by MySQL OLTP and simple ETL.

Steps
Capture source data in normalized MySQL tables (customers, orders).

Extract delta rows daily using order_date >= yesterday.

Stage raw rows; apply type normalization and null checks.

Transform into clean facts (fct_orders) and dimensions (dim_customers).

Aggregate daily revenue and LTV with GROUP BY queries.

Load aggregates into reporting tables (daily_revenue, customer_ltv).

Serve BI dashboards; schedule jobs; alert on anomalies.

sql
-- Customer LTV table
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS customer_ltv (
  customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  purchase_count INT NOT NULL,
  total_spent DECIMAL(12,2) NOT NULL,
  last_purchase DATETIME
);

INSERT INTO customer_ltv (customer_id, purchase_count, total_spent, last_purchase)
SELECT c.customer_id,
       COUNT(o.order_id) AS purchase_count,
       COALESCE(SUM(o.amount), 0) AS total_spent,
       MAX(o.order_date) AS last_purchase
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o
  ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
  AND o.status = 'PAID'
GROUP BY c.customer_id
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE
  purchase_count = VALUES(purchase_count),
  total_spent = VALUES(total_spent),
  last_purchase = VALUES(last_purchase);
Outcomes
Operational insight: Daily revenue trend, active vs. inactive customers.

Marketing action: Target customers with high browsing/low purchases.

ML readiness: Features (LTV, recency, frequency) ready for churn/upsell models.
